WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:02.908
MyCampusView Academy.

2
00:00:02.958 --> 00:00:06.390
AI for Young Builders, video 8.

3
00:00:06.440 --> 00:00:10.770
Decision trees: AI that asks questions.

4
00:00:11.950 --> 00:00:17.555
Remember the game where you guess an animal by asking yes or no questions?

5
00:00:17.605 --> 00:00:21.070
A decision tree is an AI that plays that game.

6
00:00:22.450 --> 00:00:28.926
It starts with one question at the top, called the root.

7
00:00:28.976 --> 00:00:30.441
Has feathers?

8
00:00:30.491 --> 00:00:32.539
Yes: it is a bird.

9
00:00:32.589 --> 00:00:33.938
No: has fur?

10
00:00:33.988 --> 00:00:35.336
Yes: mammal.

11
00:00:35.386 --> 00:00:36.385
No: fish.

12
00:00:36.435 --> 00:00:41.280
The answers at the ends are called leaves.

13
00:00:42.650 --> 00:00:45.590
The skill is choosing good questions.

14
00:00:45.640 --> 00:00:49.145
Has feathers splits out the birds perfectly.

15
00:00:49.195 --> 00:00:55.043
Is it brown is useless, because birds, fish and mammals can all be brown.

16
00:00:55.093 --> 00:01:01.830
Computers build trees by trying every question and keeping the one that splits best.

17
00:01:03.150 --> 00:01:06.354
Now test your tree on tricky animals.

18
00:01:06.404 --> 00:01:09.783
A whale lives in water but is a mammal.

19
00:01:09.833 --> 00:01:12.949
A penguin is a bird that cannot fly.

20
00:01:12.999 --> 00:01:15.411
A bat flies but is a mammal.

21
00:01:15.461 --> 00:01:21.830
A tree that is perfect on its training animals can still trip over these.

22
00:01:23.150 --> 00:01:27.666
Decision trees have a superpower: you can read every step.

23
00:01:27.716 --> 00:01:32.310
That means people can check them and spot unfair questions.

24
00:01:32.360 --> 00:01:38.292
So they are used where decisions affect people, like deciding which patients

25
00:01:38.342 --> 00:01:40.260
a nurse should see first.

26
00:01:41.800 --> 00:01:42.997
Let us recap.

27
00:01:43.047 --> 00:01:46.929
A decision tree asks yes-or-no questions.

28
00:01:46.979 --> 00:01:51.437
Good questions split examples into pure groups.

29
00:01:51.487 --> 00:01:54.698
You can read and check every step.

30
00:01:54.748 --> 00:01:59.110
Next: Teaching AI to read: words and feelings.
